  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nAn upper level low pressure system will lift northeast across the \nupper Mississippi Valley and western Great Lakes tonight into \nWednesday. As it does, an embedded mid level short wave rotating \naround the broader low will lift northeast into the mid Ohio Valley.\nThis will be accompanied by increasing southerly flow and a\ndeveloping 40-50 knot 850 mb jet that will shift east across our \narea late tonight into Wednesday. Good moisture transport will advect\ninto our area with PWs pushing up to around 1.5 inches or so through\nthe day on Wednesday. This should all lead to widespread showers \ndeveloping later tonight across our southwest/west and then \noverspreading the rest of our area through Wednesday morning. Pcpn \nwill the continue through the afternoon before tapering off from the\nwest Wednesday night. Some weak instabilities will also be pulled up\ninto our area so a few embedded thunderstorms will be possible, \nespecially across southern portions of our area. Deep layer shear \nwill be strong, but any severe threat should be limited by the lack \nof better instabilities. Locally heavy rainfall will also be \npossible given the increasing PWs, with the heaviest rainfall amounts\nexpected mainly along and south of the Ohio River. Storm total \namounts of 1 to 1.5 inches will be possible in spots across our\nsouthwest, but given how dry we have been the last few weeks, think \nthe overall flood threat will remain on the low side. \n\nKey message 2) \nWe will transition to more of a west to northwest flow pattern aloft\nlater this week and into early next week. A series of mid level \nshort waves dropping down across the Great Lakes will likely lead to \noccasional lower end chances for showers Friday through Sunday. \nTemperatures will trend back to seasonable readings with highs over \nthe weekend and into early next week generally in the 55 to 60 degree\nrange.",
